    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d413">

                <pb facs="0597" n="311" xml:id="pg311"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857/223</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-11-20T16:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 20, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received November 21, 5 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4619. Department’s 3538,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d413-f1"/> November 2, 2 [5] p. m.
                    The following reply has been received from the Foreign Office:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>The undersigned has the honor to make the following reply to the note of Mr.
                            Joseph Clark Grew, Chargé
                        d’Affaires of the United States of America dated the 5th instant relative to
                        the reported sinking of the English steamers <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Rievaulx Abbey</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">Strathtay</hi> and the
                        Dutch [Danish] steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Antwerpian</hi> by German
                        submarines; according to the investigation of the Admiralty Staff of the
                        Imperial German Navy no attack was made by German naval forces on the
                        English steamers <hi rendition="#italic">Rievaulx Abbey</hi> and <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Strathtay</hi>. It would therefore be of interest to
                        the German Government to learn what basis there is for the report which
                        reached the Department of State of the United States of America that these
                        steamers were sunk by German submarines.</p>
                    <p>The Dutch [Danish] steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Antwerpen</hi> (not <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Antwerpian</hi>) was captured by a German submarine
                        in cruiser warfare on September 12 last for carrying contraband and sunk in
                        compliance with the rules of the Declaration of London. The crew was first
                        given the opportunity to reach a place of safety.</p>
                    <p>The undersigned avails himself [etc.]</p>
                    <p rendition="#right">
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Von Jagow</hi>
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Foreign Office</hi>, <lb/>
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1916-11-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 19, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
